   "title": "Congress leader seeks action against Chief Secretaries of six States for skipping PAC hearing",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/congress-leader-seeks-action-against-chief-secretaries-of-six-states-for-skipping-pac-hearing/article71620836.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:19:52 +0530",
   "_dt": 1792860592.0,
   "ageDays": 0.04,
   "summary": "Venugopal, has written to Lok Sabha Speaker Om Birla seeking action against the Chief Secretaries of six States for not appearing before the panel despite being summoned.",
   "summary_zh": "韦努戈帕尔致函人民院议长奥姆·比尔拉，请求就6个邦的首席秘书未按传唤要求出席小组调查一事采取行动。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The Public Accounts Committee (PAC) chairperson and senior Congress leader, K.C. Venugopal, has written to Lok Sabha Speaker Om Birla seeking action against the Chief Secretaries of six States for not appearing before the panel despite being summoned. Also read | PAC grills Centre, States on ‘fraud markers’ flagged by CAG in SC scholarship schemes In a letter to the Lok Sabha Speaker dated October 22, Mr. Venugopal flagged the absence of the Chief Secretaries from Uttar Pradesh, Karnataka, Punjab, Odisha, Bihar and Madhya Pradesh at a hearing on irregularities in scholarship schemes for students belonging to Scheduled Castes. The Congress leader noted that the panel, which was examining pre-matric and post-matric scholarship programmes for students belonging to Scheduled Castes, had found delays in onboarding States on the national scholarship portal, gaps in coordination between the Centre and States, and problems in disbursement of funds during a study visit to Jaipur in September. Communications went to the six States on October 8 and 9, asking Chief Secretaries and officials of the departments concerned to appear before the PAC in connection with the irregularities, Mr. Venugopal said in the letter. However, none of the six Chief Secretaries turned up at the meeting, though exemptions were not granted, he noted. Secretaries of the Social Welfare Departments of Karnataka and Punjab and the Education Secretary of Bihar appeared and gave evidence along with representatives of the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment. The Madhya Pradesh Education Secretary arrived as scheduled, and the Committee decided to call the State again. Mr. Venugopal also asked the Speaker to act against the Social Welfare Secretaries of Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, and Odisha. All members of the PAC took note of the matter and the committee held that the absence amounts to breach of privilege, Mr. Venugopal said in the letter, urging the Speaker to take appropriate actions for the violation. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
